cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "fabric-wt-harness: an independent WebTransport end for testing the fabric's own"
abstract: >
  A Python WebTransport client and server, built on aioquic, used to test the
  Godot HTTP/3 and WebTransport implementation in fabric-godot-core by swapping
  roles. It exists because two ends of one implementation agree with each other
  about anything both get wrong: a Godot client against a Godot server passes
  every test the pair can run and then fails against a browser. This end was
  written from the specification instead, so a disagreement between them means
  something.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-12"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/fabric-wt-harness"
keywords:
  - webtransport
  - http3
  - quic
  - conformance-testing
  - aioquic
  - godot

references:

  - type: software
    title: "aioquic: a QUIC and HTTP/3 implementation in Python"
    authors:
      - family-names: "André"
        given-names: Jeremy
      - name: "aioquic contributors"
    year: 2026
    version: "1.3.0"
    license: BSD-3-Clause
    url: "https://github.com/aiortc/aioquic"
    notes: >
      The independent end. Chosen because it implements HTTP/3 and WebTransport
      from the specifications rather than from the Godot side, which is the only
      property that makes a disagreement informative.

  - type: software
    title: "fabric-godot-core: the Godot engine fork, branch gyre"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/fabric-godot-core"
    notes: >
      What is under test. modules/http3 carries WebTransportPeer over picoquic,
      and modules/http3/demo holds the Godot half of the role swap.

  - type: software
    title: "picoquic: a minimal QUIC implementation"
    authors:
      - family-names: Huitema
        given-names: Christian
    year: 2026
    license: MIT
    url: "https://github.com/private-octopus/picoquic"
    notes: "What the Godot end runs on, vendored inside fabric-godot-core."

  # ── The specifications both ends are held to ─────────────────────────────────
  #
  # Named because the point of two implementations is that neither is the
  # standard. When they disagree, these decide which one is wrong.

  - type: standard
    title: "RFC 9114: HTTP/3"
    authors:
      - family-names: Bishop
        given-names: Mike
    year: 2022
    url: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114"

  - type: standard
    title: "RFC 9297: HTTP Datagrams and the Capsule Protocol"
    authors:
      - family-names: Schinazi
        given-names: David
      - family-names: Pauly
        given-names: Tommy
    year: 2022
    url: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9297"
    notes: "The datagram path, which is the unreliable-unsequenced half of what WT offers."

  - type: standard
    title: "RFC 9220: Bootstrapping WebSockets with HTTP/3"
    authors:
      - family-names: Hamilton
        given-names: Ryan
    year: 2022
    url: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9220"
    notes: "Extended CONNECT, which is how a WebTransport session is opened."
